e it can also be thing  could be lonely time and it's also when  families get together and things come to  a head and a lot of people who've got  PTSD you know can often the Christmas  can be a kick starter for that and it is  shocking really isn't it when you think  and when you when you talk to homeless  people did a proportion of them are  servicemen because you know they leave  this institution  that is regimented that is organized and  all of a sudden they find themselves on  their own was on the guys I was talking  to you today you know he was involved in  a serious motorcycle accident after it  oh why was it serving but after he got  back from Afghanistan he left the army  he fed himself on his own in virtually  any end he had 15 pence to his name but  through working with healthy Heroes he's  got himself back together again and you  see them light fitter than I am and he's  a fantastic guy and also you could at  bear in mind a lot of people who've  served they have skills hmm and so many  of them are unemployed we need to get  them back to get them back into society  to integrate them back in we have to  help them get jobs  so just for a hundred and fifty pounds  you can get someone get them trained how  to write a CV properly so they can you  know apply for a job yeah again you sort  of feel isn't that the role of the  military and the mo D to support someone  as they make that transition into  civilian life I wonder also whether  public awareness is starting to wane now  that we're not involved in the big  campaigns like Iraq and Afghanistan but  of course and I think everybody that  went out to Afghan knew that was gonna  happen but it's not just gonna help  people who've been Afghans on it's  anyone that's served wherever they may  observe and of course you know it's not  headline news anymore some people have  very short memories often politicians  have very short memories it's not  fashionable  maybe but you know as you say seven  people a day leaving 